Which one of the following is the most
likely cause of the problem?','{"A":"\\diopathic.","B":"Anormal physiological change.","C":"Testicular neoplasm.","D":"Obesity.","E":"Drug-related.\nent\nMCQ 2.136\nFour days ago a 7-year-old boy injured his left leg falling out of a tree. During the\npast two days he has been generally ‘off colour’ and has had a painful left leg.\nApart from uncomplicated minor childhood illnesses, he had been previously\nhealthy. The only positive findings on examination are a temperature of 38:4°C;\nslight swelling and moderate tenderness localised over the medial aspect of his left\ntibia 4cm below his knee, a few bruises over both shins, and a soft systolic cardiac\nmurmur. Which one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?\nA. Acute rheumatic fever.\nB. Acute leukaemia.\nC. Greenstick fracture.\nD. Osteomyelitis.\nE. Anaphylactoid purpura.\neee\nMCQ 2.137\nA patient with a three-month history of anorexia and weight loss is found to have\na carcinoma of the stomach. Surgery involving a gastric resection has been\nrecommended. The patient has been advised by the attending surgeon that as he,\nthe surgeon, is about to go overseas for a week, the operation will be deferred until\nthe specialist returns, as the surgeon would feel uncomfortable not being available\nin the immediate postoperative period. The surgeon has advised the patient that a\n10-day delay in the surgery would make no difference to his outcome. The patient\nbecomes quite upset and is not satisfied with what he sees as an unnecessary\ndelay in his treatment. As the referring doctor, which one of the following is the most\nappropriate management option advice?\nA. Refer the patient to another surgeon.\nB. Suggest the patient gets a legal opinion on the inappropriate delay.\nC. Contact the hospital patient advocate.\nD. Have the patient admitted via the Emergency Department.\nE. Reinforce the specialist''s advice.\n91"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marsh