MCQ 2.142
A 3-year-old boy is seen because of the onset of nonspecific central abdominal
pain of two days duration. On examination, he has a large firm mass just to the left
of the midline in the upper abdomen. He is also noted to be hypertensive and, on
urine testing, he has microscopic haematuria. On further careful examination the
left side of his body is noted to be slightly larger than the right side. Which one of the
following is the most likely cause for this mass?
A. Neuroblastoma.
B. Splenomegaly.
C. Polycystic kidney disease.
D. Wilms tumour.
E. Left-sided hydronephrosis.
SESS
MCQ 2.143
A 6-month-old female infant is found to have a heart murmur at the routine
sixemonth check. She has previously been healthy apart from infantile colic. Her
weight is on the 25th percentile, height is on the 10th percentile, she is not cyanosed,
has no chest deformity and her peripheral pulses are normal. There is a palpable
thrill at the left sternal edge. The apex beat is palpable in the fifth intercostal space
on the midclavicular line. She has a grade 4/6 systolic cardiac murmur audible
maximally at the left sternal edge, radiating to the left axilla and into the back.
There is no variation with respiration or posture change. Which one of the following is
the most likely diagnosis?','{"A":"Fallot tetralogy.","B":"Mitral stenosis.","C":"Atrial septal defect (ASD).","D":"Ventricular septal defect (VSD).","E":"Benign flow murmur.\n93"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[109],"source_qid":"2.141","duplicate_group_id":"exact-4d55c4f17c5d","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"1ed10b327cf2d2461860","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":109,"source_qid":"2.141"}],"parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_multi_group","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"1ed10b327cf2d2461860","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":109,"supporting_pages":[109],"source_qid":"2.141","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"He has responded to indomethacin aie i hea\n50mg t.d.s. and his serum uric acid taken |\nat the time of presentation was 0.56mmol/L | oi A alll\n(0.14-0.42). Which one of the following is the\nmost appropriate next step in treatment?\nA. Cease indomethacin and prescribe\nallopurinol 300mg daily.\nB. Aspirate the joint to confirm the diagnosis.\nC. Continue his indomethacin for three months.\nD. X-ray his foot to exclude other joint pathology.\nE. Reduce his medication over one week and review.\nent Sls SESS\nMCQ 2.142\nA 3-year-old boy is seen because of the onset of nonspecific central abdominal\npain of two days duration. On examination, he has a large firm mass just to the left\nof the midline in the upper abdomen. 