Section 2 - Trial Examination
Se
MCQ 2.141
A 52-year-old man presented four days ago ,
when a diagnosis was made of typical acute ie: °
gout in his left great toe metatarsophalangeal
joint as illustrated. He has no past history of joint rd
problems. He has responded to indomethacin aie i hea
50mg t.d.s. and his serum uric acid taken |
at the time of presentation was 0.56mmol/L | oi A alll
(0.14-0.42). Which one of the following is the
most appropriate next step in treatment?
A. Cease indomethacin and prescribe
allopurinol 300mg daily.
B. Aspirate the joint to confirm the diagnosis.
C. Continue his indomethacin for three months.
D. X-ray his foot to exclude other joint pathology.
E. Reduce his medication over one week and review.
ent Sls SESS
MCQ 2.142
A 3-year-old boy is seen because of the onset of nonspecific central abdominal
pain of two days duration. On examination, he has a large firm mass just to the left
of the midline in the upper abdomen. He is also noted to be hypertensive and, on
urine testing, he has microscopic haematuria. On further careful examination the
left side of his body is noted to be slightly larger than the right side. Which one of the
following is the most likely cause for this mass?','{"A":"Neuroblastoma.","B":"Splenomegaly.","C":"Polycystic kidney disease.","D":"Wilms tumour.","E":"Left-sided hydronephrosis.\nSESS\nMCQ 2.143"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[109],"source_qid":"2.141","duplicate_group_id":"exact-103451802b18","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"48ff7c366d14db8029b5","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":109,"source_qid":"2.141"}],"parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_multi_group","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"48ff7c366d14db8029b5","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":109,"supporting_pages":[109],"source_qid":"2.141","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"Australian Medical Council\nSection 2 - Trial Examination\nSe\nMCQ 2.141\nA 52-year-old man presented four days ago ,\nwhen a diagnosis was made of typical acute ie: °\ngout in his left great toe metatarsophalangeal\njoint as illustrated. He has no past history of joint rd\nproblems. He has responded to indomethacin aie i hea\n50mg t.d.s. and his serum uric acid taken |\nat the time of presentation was 0.56mmol/L | oi A alll\n(0.14-0.42). Which one of the following is the\nmost appropriate next step in treatment?\nA. Cease indomethacin and prescribe\nallopurinol 300mg daily.\nB. Aspirate the joint to confirm the diagnosis.\nC. Continue his indomethacin for three months.\nD. X-ray his foot to exclude ot