stion 3— Adult Health and Aged Care
MCQ 3.054
A 20-year-old man is brought to the Emergency Department
following a head-on motor car accident in which he was the
front seat passenger. His only injury involves his left leg, which is
seen to be shortened, flexed at the hip and internally rotated,
as illustrated. Which one of the following is the most likely
diagnosis?
Central dislocation of the hip.
Fractured neck of femur.
. Fractured pelvis.
Fractured shaft of femur.
Posterior dislocation of the hip.
MmOQ2>
MCQ 3.055
The 25-year-old man pictured injured
his right shoulder in a tackle while
playing rugby, and subsequently
developed severe shoulder pain and
difficulty in lifting his right arm above
shoulder level. The appearance on
examination is shown. Which one
of the following is the most likely
diagnosis?','{"A":"Dislocated glenohumeral joint.","B":"Rupture of the shoulder rotator\ncuff tendon.","C":"Fracture of the surgical neck of\nhumerus.","D":"Rupture of the long head of biceps tendon.","E":"Dislocated acromioclavicular (AC) joint.\n\nMCQ 3.056\n\nA 45-year-old man limps into the clinic. While playing squash he had felt a sudden\npain at the back of his right ankle, as if his opponent had kicked him. On examination,\nwhich one of the following would be the most likely finding?\n\nA localised bruising of the calf.\n\nLocalised tenderness over the lower end of the fibula.\n. Pain on passive movement of the ankle joint.\nWeakness of plantar flexion of the foot.\n\nX-ray evidence of a stress fracture of the tibia.\n\nMOQOP>"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.054","duplicate_group_id":"exact-44d71098528b","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"38b6d10a5397435624ec","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":246,"source_qid":"3.054"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"38b6d10a5397435624ec","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":246,"source_qid":"3.054","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"stralian Medical Council\nstion 3— Adult Health and Aged Care\nMCQ 3.054\nA 20-year-old man is brought to the Emergency Department\nfollowing a head-on motor car accident in which he was the\nfront seat passenger. His only injury involves his left leg, which is\nseen to be shortened, flexed at the hip and internally rotated,\nas illustrated. Which one of the following is the most likely\ndiagnosis?\nCentral dislocation of the hip.\nFractured neck of femur.\n. Fractured pelvis.\nFractured shaft of femur.\nPosterior dislocation of the hip.\nMmOQ2>\nMCQ 3.055\nThe 25-year-old man pictured injured\nhis right shoulder in a tackle while\nplaying rugby, and subsequently\ndeveloped severe shoulder pain and\ndifficulty in lifting his right arm above\nshoulder level. The appearance on\nexamination is shown. Which one\nof the following is the most likely\ndiagnosis?","options":{"A":"Dislocated glenohumeral joint.","B":"Rupture of the shoulder rotator\ncuff tendon.","C":"Fracture of the surgical neck of\nhumerus.","D":"Rupture of the long head of biceps tendon.","E":"Dislocated acromioclavicular (AC) joint.\n\nMCQ 3.056\n\nA 45-year-old man limps into the clinic. While playing squash he had felt a sudden\npain at the back of his right ankle, as if his opponent had kicked him. On examination,\nwhich one of the following would be the most likely finding?\n\nA localised bruising of the calf.\n\nLocalised tenderness over the lower end of the fibula.\n. Pain on passive movement of the ankle joint.\nWeakness of plantar flexion of the foot.\n\nX-ray evidence of a stress fracture of the tibia.\n\nMOQOP>"},"source_answer":null,"source_explanation":null,"source_answer_status":"pending_import","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"medium","currentness_keywords":["age"],"likely_visual_question":true,"parser_confidence":"high","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract","parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","stem_hash":"44d71098528bb5a4031cd380f7ebcd70039e2758","duplicate_group_id":"exact-44d71098528b","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"38b6d10a5397435624ec","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbo