Section 3 — Adult Health and Aged Care
MCQ 3.066
An 80-year old man is admitted with acute confusion
for the preceding three days. He has a background
of hypertension, chronic renal failure, atrial fibrillation
on warfarin and Type 2 diabetes mellitus controlled
on oral hypoglycaemics. His BP is 170/90mmHg,
pulse 100/min showing irregular atrial fibrillation,
and temperature is 37.8°C. Respiratory examination
shows evidence of left lower lobe Pneumonia. He
has a left homonymous hemianopia. His non-contrast
computed tomography (CT) scan is shown. Which
one of the following is the most likely diagnosis?
A. Brain tumour.
B. Subdural haemorrhage.
C. Cerebral infarction.
D. Intracerebral haemorrhage.
Normal pressure hydrocephalus.
mo
MCQ 3.067
A 60-year-old man presented with a very painful swollen tight knee which had
developed over the previous two days. There was a past history of minor trauma to the
knee 2 years ago and he passed a kidney stone 5 years previously. He drank three to
four cans of beer daily and smoked ten cigarettes daily. He had hypertension treated
with perindopril 5mg daily and metoprolol 100mg daily. He had a temperature of
38.1°C and a painful tender effusion of the right knee. His plasma urate was 0.40mmol/L
(0.20-0.40). A joint aspiration showed clear fluid with negative birefringent crystals on
microscopy, and culture was negative. He was commenced on indomethacin 50mg
t.d.s. and improved dramatically in 48 hours. Which one of the following is the most
