Section 3-—Adult Health and Aged Care q
MCQ 3.089
A 20-year-old man attends the Emergency Department with an attack of asthma.
Which one of the following is the most valid guide in assessing the severity of his
asthma attack?
A. Severity of dyspnoea.
B. Loudness of wheezing.
C. Heart rate.
D. Forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV,).
E. Oxygen tension of arterial blood (PaO,).
MCQ 3.090
A 60-year-old man with a long history of high alcohol intake presents with ascites.
He also has a history of longstanding hypertension. In differentiating ascites caused
by congestive heart failure and that caused by cirrhosis of the liver, which one of the
following is the most useful single physical sign?','{"A":"Enlargement of the liver.","B":"Systolic murmur.","C":"Jaundice.","D":"Raised jugular venous pressure (JVP).","E":"Oedema of the ankles.\nMCQ 3.091"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[262],"source_qid":"3.089","duplicate_group_id":"exact-cb470a1bbcab","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"f6df4067da6de6dc0789","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":262,"source_qid":"3.089"}],"parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_multi_group","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','high','{"record_id":"f6df4067da6de6dc0789","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":262,"supporting_pages":[262],"source_qid":"3.089","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"Australian Medical Council 7\nSection 3-—Adult Health and Aged Care q\nMCQ 3.089\nA 20-year-old man attends the Emergency Department with an attack of asthma.\nWhich one of the following is the most valid guide in assessing the severity of his\nasthma attack?\nA. Severity of dyspnoea.\nB. Loudness of wheezing.\nC. Heart rate.\nD. Forced expiratory volume in one second (FEV,).\nE. Oxygen tension of arterial blood (PaO,).\nMCQ 3.090\nA 60-year-old man with a long history of high alcohol intake presents with ascites.\nHe also has a history of longstanding hypertension. In differentiating ascites caused\nby congestive heart failure and that caused by cirrhosis of the liver, which one of the\nfollo