INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('87519b110af68f04a3be','96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf','265','Mixed/Unclassified','ae = : Australian Medical Council
— Section 3 — Adult Health and Aged Care
MCQ 3.095
A 35-year-old woman is admitted with a haematemesis. On examination she has a BP
of 95/60mmHg and a pulse rate of 100/min. She appears reasonably well nourished.
Her sclerae are yellow and she has scratch marks and bruises on her arms. Two spider
naevi are noted on her chest. Her abdomen is distended and soft with evidence of
shifting dullness. Dilated veins are easily visible in the subcutaneous tissues and the
liver is palpable three finger-breadths below the costal margin. Her haemoglobin is
85g/L (115-165), serum albumin 28g/L (32-45) and the serum alanine aminotransferase
SOU/L (<35). Which one of her clinical and biochemical findings is likely to have the
greatest impact on her long-term prognosis?','{"A":"Her BP on admission.","B":"The serum albumin concentration. :","C":"The number of spider naevi present.","D":"The size of the liver on admission.","E":"The serum alanine aminotransferase concentration.\nMCQ 3.096\nA 56-year-old man is seen in the Emergency Department with acute upper abdominal\npain. The pain came on suddenly two hours earlier, is constant, severe and present\nin the epigastrium. He feels cold and clammy and is sweating. Movement makes\nthe pain worse and he can only take shallow breaths. He has upper abdominal\ntenderness and rebound tenderness. The patient is normally in good health and has\nnever had this pain before. He smokes 20 cigarettes a day. A recent routine check\nshowed his cholesterol was marginally elevated. Which one of the following would be\nthe most likely explanation for his symptoms?\nA. A perforated peptic ulcer.\nB. An inferior myocardial infarction.\nC. Biliary colic.\nD. Acute pancreatitis.\nE. Aruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm.\nMCQ 3.097\nA 55-year-old man presents with a six-month history of increasing dysphagia for solid\nfood. He has a previous history, going back over many years, of gastro-oesophageal\nreflux. He has managed his reflux with antacids but, since the dysphagia started,\nhis reflux has not been so troublesome. Which one of the following is the most likely\ndiagnosis?\nA. Carcinoma of the oesophagus.\nB. Achalasia of the oesophagus.\nC. Peptic oesophageal stricture.\nD. Paraoesophageal hernia.\nE. Ulcerative oesophagitis.\n247"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.095","duplicate_group_id":"exact-bbd75ffdcaaa","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"87519b110af68f04a3be","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf"