A. Her BP on admission.
B. The serum albumin concentration. :
C. The number of spider naevi present.
D. The size of the liver on admission.
E. The serum alanine aminotransferase concentration.
MCQ 3.096
A 56-year-old man is seen in the Emergency Department with acute upper abdominal
pain. The pain came on suddenly two hours earlier, is constant, severe and present
in the epigastrium. He feels cold and clammy and is sweating. Movement makes
the pain worse and he can only take shallow breaths. He has upper abdominal
tenderness and rebound tenderness. The patient is normally in good health and has
never had this pain before. He smokes 20 cigarettes a day. A recent routine check
showed his cholesterol was marginally elevated. Which one of the following would be
the most likely explanation for his symptoms?
A. A perforated peptic ulcer.
B. An inferior myocardial infarction.
C. Biliary colic.
D. Acute pancreatitis.
E. Aruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm.
MCQ 3.097
A 55-year-old man presents with a six-month history of increasing dysphagia for solid
food. He has a previous history, going back over many years, of gastro-oesophageal
reflux. He has managed his reflux with antacids but, since the dysphagia started,
his reflux has not been so troublesome. Which one of the following is the most likely
