3 Section 3 - Adult Health and Aged Care
Which one of the following was likely to be the most important factor contributing to
the deep wound dehiscence?','{"A":"The age of the patient.","B":"The paralytic ileus.","C":"The presence of the wound infection.","D":"The low haemoglobin.","E":"The low serum albumin.\nMCQ 3.115\nA 55-year-old man presents with a 24-hour history of severe and localised abdominal\npain. He points to an area above his umbilicus. There are no other symptoms and he\nis otherwise well. On examination he is a large muscular man, with a body mass index\n(BMI) of 30. There is nothing abnormal to see or feel, apart from a tender lobulated\nsubcutaneous lump half-way between the xiphisternum and the umbilicus. This lump is\nstill palpable when the patient tenses his abdominal recti. Which one of the following\nis the most likely cause of his problem?\nA. Paraumbilical hernia.\nB. Spigelian hernia.\nC. Epigastric hernia.\nD. Infected epidermoid cyst.\nE. Torsion of a lipoma.\nMCQ 3.116\nA 45-year-old woman is admitted with a two-day history of right upper quadrant\npain and fever. The pain is constant and she has been unable to sleep. The pain is\ngetting worse and she has started to vomit. There has been no change in the colour\nof the urine. On examination she looks unwell, is dehydrated and her sclera are\ntinged yellow. There is a tender mass in the right upper quadrant and the mass moves\nwith respiration. Which one of the following is the most likely underlying cause of her\nproblem?\nA. A gallstone obstructing the cystic duct.\nB. A gallstone impacted at the lower end of the (common) bile duct.\nC. A gallstone passing down the (common) bile duct.\nD. A tumour in the gallbladder.\nE. An abscess within the liver.\nMCQ 3.117\nA 63-year-old man from Northern Australia, who has had no previous surgery, presents\nwith small bowel obstruction. The hernial orifices are clear. An abdominal computed\ntomography (CT) shows that the underlying problem is a small bowel intussusception.\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of the intussusception?\nA. Enlarged Peyer patches.\nB. Crohn disease of the jejunum.\nC. Meckel diverticulum.\nD. A metastatic deposit.\nE. Lymphoma of the small bowel.\n\n253"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.115","duplicate_group_id":"exact-da3ca25192d1","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"28a4c6f2e7ae19b39202","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":271,"source_qid":"3.115"}],"parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_base_parser","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,'text',NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"28a4c6f2e7ae19b39202","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":271,"source_qid":"3.115","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"E ae Australian Medical Council\n3 Section 3 - Adult Health and Aged Care\nWhich one of the following was likely to be the most important factor contributing to\nthe deep wound dehiscence?","options":{"A":"The age of the patient.","B":"The paralytic ileus.","C":"The presence of the wound infection.","D":"The low haemoglobin.","E":"The low serum albumin.\nMCQ 3.115\nA 55-year-old man presents with a 24-hour history of severe and localised abdominal\npain. He points to an area above his umbilicus. There are no other symptoms and he\nis otherwise well. On examination he is a large muscular man, with a body mass index\n(BMI) of 30. There is nothing abnormal to see or feel, apart from a tender lobulated\nsubcutaneous lump half-way between the xiphisternum and the umbilicus. This lump is\nstill palpable when the patient tenses his abdominal recti. Which one of the following\nis the most likely cause of his problem?\nA. Paraumbilical hernia.\nB. Spigelian hernia.\nC. Epigastric hernia.\nD. Infected epidermoid cyst.\nE. Torsion of a lipoma.\nMCQ 3.116\nA 45-year-old woman is admitted with a two-day history of right upper quadrant\npain and fever. The pain is constant and she has been unable to sleep. The pain is\ngetting worse and she has started to vomit. There has been no change in the colour\nof the urine. On examination she looks unwell, is dehydrated and her sclera are\ntinged yellow. There is a tender mass in the right upper quadrant and the mass moves\nwith respiration. Which one of the following is the most likely underlying cause of her\nproblem?\nA. A gallstone obstructing the cystic duct.\nB. A gallstone impacted at the lower end of the (common) bile duct.\nC. A gallstone passing down the (common) bile duct.\nD. A tumour in the gallbladder.\nE. An abscess within the liver.\nMCQ 3.117\nA 63-year-old man from Northern Australia, who has had no previous surgery, presents\nwith small bowel obstruction. The hernial orifices are clear. An abdominal computed\ntomography (CT) shows that the underlying problem is a small bowel intussusception.\nWhich one of the following is the most likely cause of the intussusception?\nA. Enlarged Peyer patches.\nB. Crohn disease of the jejunum.\nC. Meckel diverticulum.\nD. A metastatic deposit.\nE. Lymphoma of the small bowel.\n\n253"},"source_answer":null,"source_explanation":null,"source_answer_status":"pending_import","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"medium","currentness_keywords":["age"],"likely_visual_question":false,"parser_confidence":"high","parser_mode":"v28_selective_ocr_base_parser","text_method":"v28_selective_ocr","record_type":"text_mcq","options_mode":"text","parser_version":"2.8-residual-resolver-selective-ocr-visual-2026-09-22","stem_hash":"da3ca25192d1fbf