ection 3 —- Adult Health and Aged Care','{"A":"Thyroid function.","B":"Full blood count.","C":"Fasting blood glucose.","D":"Prostate specific antigen.","E":"Creatinine, urea and electrolytes.\n\nA 20-year-old woman presents with a painless lump in the left breast which she has\nnoted for the past week. There is no other relevant history. Breast examination confirms\na discrete, firm, mobile breast lump 1.5cm in diameter. Which one of the following\ninvestigations is the most appropriate?\n\nMammography.\n\nUltrasound examination.\n\n. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI).\nSerum prolactin.\n\nXerography.\n\nMOORP>\n\nMCQ 3.134\n\nA 40-year-old woman presents with a painless swelling in the neck present for three\nmonths. On examination there is a 4cm swelling in the lower pole of the left lobe\nof the thyroid. On ultrasound the nodule is mainly cystic but with some solid areas.\nThe thyroxine (T4) level is 20pmol/L (10-25). Which one of the following is the most\nappropriate next step in management?\n\nA. Repeat ultrasound examination in six months.\n\nB. Fine needle aspiration cytology of the nodule under ultrasound.\nC. Magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) of the neck.\n\nD. Core biopsy of the thyroid nodule under stereotactic X-ray control.\nE. Thyroid lobectomy and frozen section diagnosis.\n\nA 65-year-old woman has a screening\nmammogram, her first, which shows a\nfocal area of calcification as illustrated. No\nabnormality is found on clinical examination.\nWhich one of the following is the most likely\ndiagnosis?\n\nFat necrosis.\n\nCarcinoma.\n\n. Plasma cell mastitis.\n\nFibroadenoma.\n\nCystosarcoma phyllodes.\n\nMmOQ2>"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"3.134","duplicate_group_id":"exact-3a226c6c2e7c","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"5ac5aba61d1214960e6f","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","